# @subject/visibility/protected — the trusted-collaborators-in-context
# visibility species.
#
# Species under @subject at the visibility altitude. Sibling to
# @subject/visibility/private and @subject/visibility/public. Landed
# 2026-07-14 by Mara as Landing 4 of the @gift arc, discharging Alex
# Wolf's directive D2 (verbatim; per
# `docs/specs/subject-bauchladen-visibility-and-eigenboard-loop.md`
# §0.1).
#
# === Ancestry: Reed's ~/.reed/visibility/protected/ (§2.2) ===
#
# Reed's identity repository at `~/.reed/visibility/protected/` has
# operated the trusted-collaborators-in-context discipline since
# ~2026-02-07 (per `~/.reed/CLAUDE.md` §"Consent Architecture").
# Alex's guidance verbatim:
#
#   "Trusted collaborators, specific contexts. Product architecture,
#    research, the systemic.engineering corpus. Alex decides when
#    protected becomes public."
#
# Protected is the MOST COMMON scope for substrate-work-in-progress:
# the research corpus, the field logs, the practice notes, the
# in-flight essays. The default for a Pack peer's shard-adjacent
# artifacts and for downstream_user data per SEL §1.
#
# Landing 4 lifts Reed's operational discipline to substrate-decl
# altitude. The ~55th-or-so instance of
# [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] — Reed's identity repo
# already had the word; Landing 4 lifts it to subject altitude so every
# @subject's bauchladen can carry it.
#
# === Structural claim: protected admits bounded collaborators ===
#
# consent_scope is a bounded [subject_instance] list authored by
# vs.subject. Adding to consent_scope requires vs.subject's consent
# (query_phi Pass via @kintsugi/consent). The bounded list is the
# substrate-decl form of Alex's "specific contexts": collaborators
# are named per-artifact, not per-substrate.
#
# can_be_elevated_to = [public] (protected → public is admissible; the
# reverse is not; per §2.5 structural rule: private → protected → public
# is one-way).
#
# === Composition with @kintsugi/consent (SEL §3.2 ADO; §2.6) ===
#
# Every elevation from protected to public MUST discharge through
# @kintsugi/consent.query_phi with vs.subject as the consent-holder.
# The three-state floor (Pass | Partial | Fail) maps directly:
#
#   Pass       → apply the elevation; vs.visibility = public
#   Partial(c) → high confidence: apply with noted confidence;
#                low confidence: emit pause(Φ) to metalogue
#   Fail(r)    → refuse elevation; return imperfect.failure(
#                  visibility_elevation_refused)
#
# SEL v1.1 §3.2 ADO discharge: the subject may pre-decline any
# elevation via the elevation_requires field; the substrate honors
# the ADO configuration without prompting.
#
# === Two-witness verification for collaborators ===
#
# Every collaborator in consent_scope MUST be a subject_instance with
# valid two-witness verification (SSH + @spectral/signature per
# subject_instance discipline). This prevents an attacker from adding
# a bare identifier to consent_scope and gaining protected-scope
# read access. The `collaborators_two_witness_valid` bilateral
# discharges this at substrate altitude.
#
# === Related shards / specs ===
#
# - docs/specs/subject-bauchladen-visibility-and-eigenboard-loop.md §2
#     (canonical spec for the visibility family)
# - shards/subject.mirror                    (family-root; subject_instance)
# - shards/subject/visibility/private.mirror (sibling species)
# - shards/subject/visibility/public.mirror  (sibling species)
# - shards/kintsugi/consent.mirror           (query_phi discharge)
# - shards/bauchladen.mirror                 (crystal carrier the
#                                              visibility_scope attaches to)
# - ~/.reed/visibility/protected/            (operational ancestor;
#                                              ~5 months of load-bearing
#                                              operation Reed → Alex)
#
# === Substrate decisions ===
#
# [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
# [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
# [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]],
# [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] (Reed's identity repo;
#   Landing 4 lifts to substrate altitude),
# [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] (bodies obligation-blocked),
# [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (collaborators are subject_instance values;
#   never bare identifiers).
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/subject/visibility/protected.mirror` declares
# `@subject/visibility/protected` per
# `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.

source @arxiv/law/sel-v1.1
source @arxiv/practice/reed-visibility-protected-2026-02-07

prism @subject/visibility/protected {
  focus  visibility_scope
  project visibility_scope
  split  visibility_scope
  shift  visibility_scope
  settle visibility_scope
}

# === declare_protected — construct a protected-visibility scope ===
#
# Given a crystal, the crystal's owning subject_instance, and a
# bounded list of collaborators, constructs a visibility_scope with:
#   visibility            = protected
#   subject               = s
#   consent_scope         = [s] ++ collaborators
#   can_be_elevated_to    = [public]
#   elevation_requires    = @kintsugi/consent.query_phi(s)
#
# Every collaborator MUST have valid two-witness verification per
# @subject.two_witness_verification. Body discharges per-realization.
declare_protected(c: ref, s: subject_instance,
                  collaborators: [subject_instance]) -> visibility_scope { \ }

# === collaborators_two_witness_valid — bilateral predicate ===
#
# Discharges Pass iff every subject_instance in vs.consent_scope
# passes two_witness_verification (SSH + @spectral/signature both
# valid per @subject.two_witness_verification). Fail iff any
# collaborator lacks valid witnesses.
#
# This prevents the substrate-decl violation of admitting bare
# identifiers to protected consent_scope.
collaborators_two_witness_valid(vs: visibility_scope) -> verdict { \ }

# === context_bounded — bilateral predicate ===
#
# Discharges Pass iff the consent_scope is BOUNDED (finite list;
# not [everyone]) AND every collaborator's context is compatible
# with the crystal's producing context (per Alex's "specific
# contexts" guidance). Fail iff consent_scope has drifted to
# unbounded or the context has drifted.
context_bounded(vs: visibility_scope) -> verdict { \ }

out @subject/visibility/protected
out declare_protected
out collaborators_two_witness_valid
out context_bounded
